{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-964ed61372994192", "type": "tool_result", "content": "100\tonly after K consecutive identical predictions, whereas EBD\n101\t(1)\n102\t(2)\n103\tsmooths the decision margin ∆rt = rt − rt using a fixed\n104\tEMA, ∆r̂t = α∆r̂t−1 + (1 − α)∆rt . Although PBD and EBD\n105\tsuppress short-term fluctuations, they rely on static temporal\n106\tconstraints that cannot differentiate between stable and transition states, thereby slowing adaptation to attention switches.\n107\tTherefore, as shown in Fig. 1, we propose a state-guided adaptive decision (SGAD) framework, which adaptively modulates\n108\ttemporal smoothing based on the estimated transition state.\n109\t2.2.1. Causal state detector (CSD)\n110\tGiven that attentional transitions evolve gradually over time,\n111\trelying on single-window evidence is inherently unreliable for\n112\tswitch detection [5, 15]. The CSD therefore models causal temporal dependencies across multiple preceding windows to capture the evolving attention state rather than isolated fluctuations.\n113\tAt each time step t, the EEG embedding et is combined with\n114\tpositional encoding pt as ẽt = et + pt ∈ Rd , and then projected into query, key, and value representations:\n115\tqt = ẽt WQ ,\n116\t\n117\tkt = ẽt WK ,\n118\t\n119\tvt = ẽt WV ,\n120\t\n121\t(1)\n122\t\n123\td×dk\n124\t\n125\twhere WQ , WK , WV ∈ R\n126\tare learnable projection matrices and dk = 16. To ensure causal inference, a key-value\n127\t(KV) cache maintains historical keys and values within a sliding window of length W , enabling causal multi-head attention\n128\t(MHA) [25]. The contextual feature is then computed as:\n129\t\u0012\n130\t\u0013\n131\tqt k⊤\n132\tt−W +1:t\n133\t√\n134\tht = Softmax\n135\tvt−W +1:t ,\n136\t(2)\n137\tdk\n138\twhere kt−W +1:t and vt−W +1:t denote the cached keys and values. The attention output is further processed as:\n139\tut = LN(ht + ẽt ),\n140\t\n141\tzt = LN(FFN(ut ) + ut ),\n142\t\n143\t(3)\n144\t\n145\twhere LN(·) denotes layer normalization and FFN(·) is a\n146\tposition-wise feed-forward network. The transition probability\n147\tis computed as ŝt = σ(zt ), where ŝt ∈ [0, 1] and σ(·) denotes\n148\ta sigmoid function.\n149\t2.2.2. Adaptive gating mechanism (AGM)\n150\tGuided by the transition probability ŝt , the AGM dynamically\n151\tregulates decision updates. The adaptive gate maps ŝt to a\n152\tsmoothing factor gt via a monotonic decreasing function:\n153\tgt = gmin + (gmax − gmin ) · σ(b − aŝt ),\n154\t\n155\t(4)\n156\t\n157\twhere a > 0 and b are learnable parameters controlling the\n158\tgating function. The bounds gmin = 0.05 and gmax = 0.95\n159\tconstrain the smoothing factor within a practical range to balance tracking agility and noise suppression. This formulation\n160\tensures that higher ŝt yields smaller gt , reducing system inertia and enabling rapid adaptation during attention switches,\n161\twhereas lower ŝt produces larger gt to enforce stronger temporal smoothing in stable periods. Subsequently, the recursive\n162\tsmoother updates the decision margin ∆r̂t using the current ev(2)\n163\t(1)\n164\tidence ∆rt = rt − rt and the previous state ∆r̂t−1 :\n165\t∆r̂t = gt ∆r̂t−1 + (1 − gt )∆rt .\n166\t\n167\t(5)\n168\t\n169\tBy integrating this state-guided gain into the recursive loop,\n170\tSGAD decouples the fixed temporal trade-off inherent in conventional EBD methods.\n171\t2.2.3. Training objectives\n172\tWith frozen encoders, SGAD is trained via a multi-task objective:\n173\tLSGAD = Ldec + λ1 Lstate + λ2 Lsmooth ,\n174\t(6)\n175\twhere λ1 = 0.5 and λ2 = 0.05 based on validation experiments. The primary objective for attention decoding is\n176\tLdec = BCE(σ(∆r̂t ), yt ), which optimizes the filtered decision margin. To provide additional explicit supervision for the\n177\tCSD module in capturing dynamic attention transitions, an auxiliary state loss Lstate = BCE(ŝt , st ) is introduced. Here, triangular soft labels are defined as st = max(0, 1 − |t − tsw |/R),\n178\twhere tsw denotes the ground-truth switch time and R = 5 s\n179\tavoids sparse state supervision, yielding non-zero\n180\tlabels for\n181\tP\n182\t2\n183\tabout half the windows. Finally, Lsmooth =\n184\tt (gt − gt−1 )\n185\tregularizes temporal fluctuations of the adaptive gate.\n186\t\n187\t3. Experiments\n188\t3.1. Dataset and preprocessing\n189\tExperiments were conducted on the MS-AASD dataset [26, 27]\n190\twith EEG recordings from 13 normal-hearing adults (aged\n191\t21–28 years) performing self-initiated attention switching in\n192\tmixed-speech environments. EEG was recorded at 500 Hz\n193\tusing 64 channels. In each 60-s trial, two root-mean-square\n194\t(RMS)-normalized speech segments from one of three malefemale speaker pairs were mixed at 0 dB and presented binaurally. Participants attended to one speaker and voluntarily\n195\tswitched attention between concurrent streams, reporting attention states via two-button keypresses. Across 60 trials per participant, 13 hours of data were recorded, with a mean of 3.37\n196\tattention switches per trial.\n197\tEEG signals were re-referenced to the mastoid average,\n198\tband-pass filtered (0.5–50 Hz) and decomposed into five subbands (δ, θ, α, β, γ), then downsampled to 128 Hz and normalized per channel. Speech features were extracted from the\n199\t\n200\t\fFigure 1: Architecture of the proposed SGAD-based sequential decision framework with three stages: (i) sequential input partitioning\n201\tvia sliding windows, (ii) EEG-speech matching via frozen encoders, and (iii) state-guided adaptive decision making.\n202\t\n203\tTable 1: Seen and unseen conditions of the six protocols\n204\tProtocol\n205\t\n206\tAudio\n207\t\n208\tSpeaker\n209\t\n210\tSubject\n211\t\n212\tLOTO / LOSO\n213\tLOAO / LOSAO\n214\tLOSpO / LOSSO\n215\t\n216\tSeen\n217\tUnseen\n218\tUnseen\n219\t\n220\tSeen\n221\tSeen\n222\tUnseen\n223\t\n224\tSeen / Unseen\n225\tSeen / Unseen\n226\tSeen / Unseen\n227\t\n228\t14-th layer of pretrained wav2vec 2.0 [24], reduced to 64 dimensions via PCA, and resampled to 128 Hz. Continuous EEG\n229\tand speech features were segmented into overlapping 1.0 s windows with a 0.2 s step, starting from the first annotated attention\n230\tevent in each trial. Each segment was labeled by its dominant\n231\tattention state to mitigate sensitivity to keypress timing variability. A switch window was defined when the dominant label of\n232\tthe current window differed from that of the preceding window.\n233\t\n234\t3.3. Experimental setup and evaluation metrics\n235\tAll models were implemented in PyTorch and trained on\n236\tNVIDIA V100 GPUs using AdamW (weight decay 1 × 10−4 ).\n237\tEEG and speech encoders were pretrained for 50 epochs using Lmatch (learning rate 1 × 10−3 , batch size 64, pair-swap\n238\tp = 0.5), after which they were frozen and SGAD was trained\n239\ton sequential trial data using LSGAD under identical optimization settings with a two-window burn-in. Early stopping with\n240\ta patience of 10 epochs was applied in both stages. Decision hyperparameters were selected via validation grid search\n241\tover K ∈ {2, 4, . . . , 10}, α ∈ {0.5, 0.6, . . . , 0.9}, and W ∈\n242\t{5, 10, . . . , 25}, yielding K = 5, α = 0.8, and W = 15.\n243\tAASD performance was evaluated using three metrics: decoding accuracy (Acc), switch-F1 score (Sw-F1), and switch\n244\tdetection latency (SDL). Acc measures segment-level decoding\n245\tperformance and is defined as [5]:\n246\tN\n247\t\n248\tAcc =\n249\t3.2. Evaluation protocols\n250\tAs summarized in Table 1, six protocols were designed to\n251\tassess model generalization of audio content, speaker identity, and subject information: leave-one-trial-out (LOTO) [22],\n252\tleave-one-audio-out (LOAO) [22], leave-one-speaker-pair-out\n253\t(LOSpO) [21], leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) [21], leave-onesubject-and-audio-out (LOSAO), and leave-one-subject-andspeaker-pair-out (LOSSO). Specifically, LOTO, LOAO, and\n254\tLOSpO assess subject-dependent generalization using 3-fold\n255\tcross-validation. LOTO performs random trial partitioning to\n256\tevaluate cross-trial robustness, LOAO withholds shared audio\n257\tfolds to ensure stimulus independence, and LOSpO excludes\n258\tone predefined male-female speaker pair to assess speaker independence. In contrast, LOSO, LOSAO, and LOSSO evaluate subject-independent generalization under a unified 39-fold\n259\tstructure (13 × 3 content partitions), where content partitions\n260\tare defined by audio stimuli or speaker pairs. LOSO withholds\n261\tone speaker pair per fold to ensure a fair training budget comparison, LOSAO jointly enforces unseen-subject and unseenaudio conditions, and LOSSO evaluates the most rigorous joint\n262\tsubject-speaker independence scenario. For all protocols, five\n263\ttrials per subject are reserved for validation.\n264\t\n265\t1 X\n266\tI(ŷi = yi ),\n267\tN i=1\n268\t\n269\t(7)\n270\t\n271\twhere yi and ŷi denote the ground-truth and predicted attention\n272\tlabels of the i-th window, respectively, and N is the total number of windows. To evaluate decision stability, switch detection\n273\twas formulated as an event-level task following standard EEG\n274\tevent detection paradigms [28, 29]. A true positive switch is defined as the first prediction within a 5.0 s tolerance window (the\n275\t99th percentile of validation-set detection latency) following a\n276\tground-truth switch. Sw-F1 is defined as:\n277\tSw-F1 =\n278\t\n279\t2Nmatch\n280\t,\n281\tNpred + Ngt\n282\t\n283\t(8)\n284\t\n285\twhere Nmatch , Npred , and Ngt denote the numbers of correctly matched switches, predicted switches, and ground-truth\n286\tswitches, respectively. Additionally, SDL measures switching\n287\tresponsiveness and is computed as the average delay between\n288\tmatched predicted and ground-truth switches [17]:\n289\tSDL =\n290\t\n291\t1\n292\tNmatch\n293\t\n294\tNmatch\n295\t\n296\tX\n297\t\n298\t(tpred,j − tgt,j ),\n299\t\n300\t(9)\n301\t\n302\tj=1\n303\t\n304\twhere tpred,j and tgt,j are the timestamps of the j-th matched\n305\tpredicted and ground-truth switches, respectively.\n306\t\n307\t\fTable 2: Performance comparison across six evaluation protocols, with Acc and Sw-F1 in percentage (%), and SDL in seconds (s)\n308\tDecision Framework\n309\tEncoder\n310\t\n311\tWD\n312\t\n313\tProtocol\n314\t\n315\tPBD\n316\t\n317\tEBD\n318\t\n319\tSGAD\n320\t\n321\tAcc\n322\t\n323\tSw-F1\n324\t\n325\tSDL\n326\t\n327\tAcc\n328\t\n329\tSw-F1\n330\t\n331\tSDL\n332\t\n333\tAcc\n334\t\n335\tSw-F1\n336\t\n337\tSDL\n338\t\n339\tAcc\n340\t\n341\tSw-F1\n342\t\n343\tSDL\n344\t\n345\tCNNT\n346\t\n347\tLOTO\n348\tLOAO\n349\tLOSpO\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c54c7704-7c55-4ef9-b6d2-c3ba122c790b", "uuid": "ea9f1b25-c0ea-4893-b857-c015d9bc23f9", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T14:23:04.950Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sgad_paper.txt", "content": "only after K consecutive identical predictions, whereas EBD\n(1)\n(2)\nsmooths the decision margin ∆rt = rt − rt using a fixed\nEMA, ∆r̂t = α∆r̂t−1 + (1 − α)∆rt . Although PBD and EBD\nsuppress short-term fluctuations, they rely on static temporal\nconstraints that cannot differentiate between stable and transition states, thereby slowing adaptation to attention switches.\nTherefore, as shown in Fig. 1, we propose a state-guided adaptive decision (SGAD) framework, which adaptively modulates\ntemporal smoothing based on the estimated transition state.\n2.2.1. Causal state detector (CSD)\nGiven that attentional transitions evolve gradually over time,\nrelying on single-window evidence is inherently unreliable for\nswitch detection [5, 15]. The CSD therefore models causal temporal dependencies across multiple preceding windows to capture the evolving attention state rather than isolated fluctuations.\nAt each time step t, the EEG embedding et is combined with\npositional encoding pt as ẽt = et + pt ∈ Rd , and then projected into query, key, and value representations:\nqt = ẽt WQ ,\n\nkt = ẽt WK ,\n\nvt = ẽt WV ,\n\n(1)\n\nd×dk\n\nwhere WQ , WK , WV ∈ R\nare learnable projection matrices and dk = 16. To ensure causal inference, a key-value\n(KV) cache maintains historical keys and values within a sliding window of length W , enabling causal multi-head attention\n(MHA) [25]. The contextual feature is then computed as:\n\u0012\n\u0013\nqt k⊤\nt−W +1:t\n√\nht = Softmax\nvt−W +1:t ,\n(2)\ndk\nwhere kt−W +1:t and vt−W +1:t denote the cached keys and values. The attention output is further processed as:\nut = LN(ht + ẽt ),\n\nzt = LN(FFN(ut ) + ut ),\n\n(3)\n\nwhere LN(·) denotes layer normalization and FFN(·) is a\nposition-wise feed-forward network. The transition probability\nis computed as ŝt = σ(zt ), where ŝt ∈ [0, 1] and σ(·) denotes\na sigmoid function.\n2.2.2. Adaptive gating mechanism (AGM)\nGuided by the transition probability ŝt , the AGM dynamically\nregulates decision updates. The adaptive gate maps ŝt to a\nsmoothing factor gt via a monotonic decreasing function:\ngt = gmin + (gmax − gmin ) · σ(b − aŝt ),\n\n(4)\n\nwhere a > 0 and b are learnable parameters controlling the\ngating function. The bounds gmin = 0.05 and gmax = 0.95\nconstrain the smoothing factor within a practical range to balance tracking agility and noise suppression. This formulation\nensures that higher ŝt yields smaller gt , reducing system inertia and enabling rapid adaptation during attention switches,\nwhereas lower ŝt produces larger gt to enforce stronger temporal smoothing in stable periods. Subsequently, the recursive\nsmoother updates the decision margin ∆r̂t using the current ev(2)\n(1)\nidence ∆rt = rt − rt and the previous state ∆r̂t−1 :\n∆r̂t = gt ∆r̂t−1 + (1 − gt )∆rt .\n\n(5)\n\nBy integrating this state-guided gain into the recursive loop,\nSGAD decouples the fixed temporal trade-off inherent in conventional EBD methods.\n2.2.3. Training objectives\nWith frozen encoders, SGAD is trained via a multi-task objective:\nLSGAD = Ldec + λ1 Lstate + λ2 Lsmooth ,\n(6)\nwhere λ1 = 0.5 and λ2 = 0.05 based on validation experiments. The primary objective for attention decoding is\nLdec = BCE(σ(∆r̂t ), yt ), which optimizes the filtered decision margin. To provide additional explicit supervision for the\nCSD module in capturing dynamic attention transitions, an auxiliary state loss Lstate = BCE(ŝt , st ) is introduced. Here, triangular soft labels are defined as st = max(0, 1 − |t − tsw |/R),\nwhere tsw denotes the ground-truth switch time and R = 5 s\navoids sparse state supervision, yielding non-zero\nlabels for\nP\n2\nabout half the windows. Finally, Lsmooth =\nt (gt − gt−1 )\nregularizes temporal fluctuations of the adaptive gate.\n\n3. Experiments\n3.1. Dataset and preprocessing\nExperiments were conducted on the MS-AASD dataset [26, 27]\nwith EEG recordings from 13 normal-hearing adults (aged\n21–28 years) performing self-initiated attention switching in\nmixed-speech environments. EEG was recorded at 500 Hz\nusing 64 channels. In each 60-s trial, two root-mean-square\n(RMS)-normalized speech segments from one of three malefemale speaker pairs were mixed at 0 dB and presented binaurally. Participants attended to one speaker and voluntarily\nswitched attention between concurrent streams, reporting attention states via two-button keypresses. Across 60 trials per participant, 13 hours of data were recorded, with a mean of 3.37\nattention switches per trial.\nEEG signals were re-referenced to the mastoid average,\nband-pass filtered (0.5–50 Hz) and decomposed into five subbands (δ, θ, α, β, γ), then downsampled to 128 Hz and normalized per channel. Speech features were extracted from the\n\n\fFigure 1: Architecture of the proposed SGAD-based sequential decision framework with three stages: (i) sequential input partitioning\nvia sliding windows, (ii) EEG-speech matching via frozen encoders, and (iii) state-guided adaptive decision making.\n\nTable 1: Seen and unseen conditions of the six protocols\nProtocol\n\nAudio\n\nSpeaker\n\nSubject\n\nLOTO / LOSO\nLOAO / LOSAO\nLOSpO / LOSSO\n\nSeen\nUnseen\nUnseen\n\nSeen\nSeen\nUnseen\n\nSeen / Unseen\nSeen / Unseen\nSeen / Unseen\n\n14-th layer of pretrained wav2vec 2.0 [24], reduced to 64 dimensions via PCA, and resampled to 128 Hz. Continuous EEG\nand speech features were segmented into overlapping 1.0 s windows with a 0.2 s step, starting from the first annotated attention\nevent in each trial. Each segment was labeled by its dominant\nattention state to mitigate sensitivity to keypress timing variability. A switch window was defined when the dominant label of\nthe current window differed from that of the preceding window.\n\n3.3. Experimental setup and evaluation metrics\nAll models were implemented in PyTorch and trained on\nNVIDIA V100 GPUs using AdamW (weight decay 1 × 10−4 ).\nEEG and speech encoders were pretrained for 50 epochs using Lmatch (learning rate 1 × 10−3 , batch size 64, pair-swap\np = 0.5), after which they were frozen and SGAD was trained\non sequential trial data using LSGAD under identical optimization settings with a two-window burn-in. Early stopping with\na patience of 10 epochs was applied in both stages. Decision hyperparameters were selected via validation grid search\nover K ∈ {2, 4, . . . , 10}, α ∈ {0.5, 0.6, . . . , 0.9}, and W ∈\n{5, 10, . . . , 25}, yielding K = 5, α = 0.8, and W = 15.\nAASD performance was evaluated using three metrics: decoding accuracy (Acc), switch-F1 score (Sw-F1), and switch\ndetection latency (SDL). Acc measures segment-level decoding\nperformance and is defined as [5]:\nN\n\nAcc =\n3.2. Evaluation protocols\nAs summarized in Table 1, six protocols were designed to\nassess model generalization of audio content, speaker identity, and subject information: leave-one-trial-out (LOTO) [22],\nleave-one-audio-out (LOAO) [22], leave-one-speaker-pair-out\n(LOSpO) [21], leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) [21], leave-onesubject-and-audio-out (LOSAO), and leave-one-subject-andspeaker-pair-out (LOSSO). Specifically, LOTO, LOAO, and\nLOSpO assess subject-dependent generalization using 3-fold\ncross-validation. LOTO performs random trial partitioning to\nevaluate cross-trial robustness, LOAO withholds shared audio\nfolds to ensure stimulus independence, and LOSpO excludes\none predefined male-female speaker pair to assess speaker independence. In contrast, LOSO, LOSAO, and LOSSO evaluate subject-independent generalization under a unified 39-fold\nstructure (13 × 3 content partitions), where content partitions\nare defined by audio stimuli or speaker pairs. LOSO withholds\none speaker pair per fold to ensure a fair training budget comparison, LOSAO jointly enforces unseen-subject and unseenaudio conditions, and LOSSO evaluates the most rigorous joint\nsubject-speaker independence scenario. For all protocols, five\ntrials per subject are reserved for validation.\n\n1 X\nI(ŷi = yi ),\nN i=1\n\n(7)\n\nwhere yi and ŷi denote the ground-truth and predicted attention\nlabels of the i-th window, respectively, and N is the total number of windows. To evaluate decision stability, switch detection\nwas formulated as an event-level task following standard EEG\nevent detection paradigms [28, 29]. A true positive switch is defined as the first prediction within a 5.0 s tolerance window (the\n99th percentile of validation-set detection latency) following a\nground-truth switch. Sw-F1 is defined as:\nSw-F1 =\n\n2Nmatch\n,\nNpred + Ngt\n\n(8)\n\nwhere Nmatch , Npred , and Ngt denote the numbers of correctly matched switches, predicted switches, and ground-truth\nswitches, respectively. Additionally, SDL measures switching\nresponsiveness and is computed as the average delay between\nmatched predicted and ground-truth switches [17]:\nSDL =\n\n1\nNmatch\n\nNmatch\n\nX\n\n(tpred,j − tgt,j ),\n\n(9)\n\nj=1\n\nwhere tpred,j and tgt,j are the timestamps of the j-th matched\npredicted and ground-truth switches, respectively.\n\n\fTable 2: Performance comparison across six evaluation protocols, with Acc and Sw-F1 in percentage (%), and SDL in seconds (s)\nDecision Framework\nEncoder\n\nWD\n\nProtocol\n\nPBD\n\nEBD\n\nSGAD\n\nAcc\n\nSw-F1\n\nSDL\n\nAcc\n\nSw-F1\n\nSDL\n\nAcc\n\nSw-F1\n\nSDL\n\nAcc\n\nSw-F1\n\nSDL\n\nCNNT\n\nLOTO\nLOAO\nLOSpO", "numLines": 250, "startLine": 100, "totalLines": 782}}}
