{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-af16a31caac384aa", "type": "tool_result", "content": "593\t1.39\n594\t1.25\n595\t1.46\n596\t1.49\n597\t1.31\n598\t\n599\t76.7\n600\t75.2\n601\t75.8\n602\t76.1\n603\t75.9\n604\t\n605\t62.3\n606\t48.5\n607\t56.8\n608\t53.7\n609\t60.4\n610\t\n611\t1.42\n612\t1.28\n613\t1.49\n614\t1.52\n615\t1.32\n616\t\n617\tformance, underscoring the importance of learnable gating for\n618\tadaptive smoothing. Furthermore, eliminating the auxiliary loss\n619\tLstate reduces performance due to the lack of explicit transition\n620\tsupervision, while removing Lsmooth similarly leads to performance degradation by weakening temporal regularization. Collectively, these findings highlight the complementary roles of\n621\ttemporal context, adaptive gating, and auxiliary supervision,\n622\tconfirming the importance of explicit transition-state modeling\n623\tfor robust sequential decision-making.\n624\t4.2. Comparison of evaluation protocols\n625\t\n626\t4. Results and Discussion\n627\t4.1. Performance and ablation analysis\n628\tTable 2 compares SGAD with three baseline decision frameworks across two encoders and six evaluation protocols. WD\n629\tachieves the lowest SDL of 0.89 s by operating independently\n630\ton each window, but its Sw-F1 remains low at 29.0%, indicating limited robustness in tracking attention transitions. PBD\n631\tand EBD improve temporal consistency, increasing the mean\n632\tAcc from 77.4% to 78.6% and 79.0%, respectively, while raising Sw-F1 to 45.6% and 53.7%. However, their fixed smoothing increases system inertia, resulting in higher SDL values of\n633\t1.23 s and 1.38 s. In contrast, SGAD achieves a better tradeoff among Acc, Sw-F1, and SDL, improving the mean Acc to\n634\t79.8% and Sw-F1 to 67.3% while maintaining a lower SDL\n635\tof 1.18 s compared with EBD. This balanced improvement is\n636\tconsistently observed across all experimental settings. Overall, incorporating transition-state information into the recursive\n637\tupdate enables SGAD to enhance switch detection performance\n638\twithout the substantial latency penalty inherent in fixed smoothing, thereby enabling more adaptive decision-making.\n639\tTable 3 reports the ablation results under the LOSSO protocol. Replacing the CSD with a single-window state output markedly reduces Sw-F1 (e.g., from 62.3% to 48.5% for\n640\tFCTNet), while achieving a lower SDL of 1.28 s, highlighting the critical role of temporal context in transition estimation. Removing the AGM and substituting the learnable gate\n641\twith a fixed mapping based on (1 − ŝt ) also degrades per-\n642\t\n643\tPerformance variations across the six protocols demonstrate the\n644\timpact of generalization constraints on decoding performance.\n645\tIn both subject-dependent and subject-independent settings, accuracy declines as additional constraints on audio content and\n646\tspeaker identity are imposed. For example, under subjectdependent evaluation, the accuracy of FCTNet with the SGAD\n647\tframework decreases from 85.6% (LOTO) to 82.9% (LOAO)\n648\tand 80.5% (LOSpO), with a comparable pattern observed under\n649\tsubject-independent evaluation. Notably, LOSSO, which we introduce as the most stringent protocol, jointly enforces unseensubject and unseen-speaker conditions, resulting in the lowest\n650\taccuracy of 76.7%. These results highlight the increased difficulty of AASD under realistic cross-condition generalization\n651\tand emphasize the importance of rigorous evaluation protocols\n652\tfor practical neuro-steered hearing systems.\n653\t\n654\t5. Conclusion\n655\tThis study addresses decision instability and evaluation bias in\n656\tdynamic AASD by introducing a state-guided adaptive decision\n657\t(SGAD) framework together with structured hierarchical evaluation protocols. By adaptively modulating temporal smoothing according to inferred transition states, SGAD mitigates the\n658\ttrade-off between stability and responsiveness. The consistent\n659\tperformance degradation observed under increasingly stringent\n660\tprotocols further highlights the importance of comprehensive\n661\tevaluation across audio, speaker, and subject dimensions. Together, these contributions advance the development of reliable\n662\tneuro-steered hearing systems in dynamic environments.\n663\t\n664\t\f6. Acknowledgments\n665\tThis work was supported by the National Key Research\n666\tand Development Program of China (2025YFF0518003,\n667\t2023YFF1203502), the National Natural Science Foundation of\n668\tChina (62371217), and the Center for Computational Science\n669\tand Engineering at Southern University of Science and Technology.\n670\t\n671\t7. Generative AI Use Disclosure\n672\tAll (co-)authors are responsible and accountable for the work\n673\tand content of the paper, and consent to its submission. Generative AI tools were used for editing and polishing manuscripts,\n674\tbut were not used for producing a significant part of the\n675\tmanuscript.\n676\t\n677\t8. References\n678\t[1] E. C. Cherry, “Some experiments on the recognition of speech,\n679\twith one and with two ears,” Journal of the Acoustical Society of\n680\tAmerica, vol. 25, no. 5, pp. 975–979, 1953.\n681\t[2] N. Ding and J. Z. Simon, “Neural coding of continuous speech in\n682\tauditory cortex during monaural and dichotic listening,” Journal\n683\tof Neurophysiology, vol. 107, no. 1, pp. 78–89, 2012.\n684\t[3] S. Haykin and Z. Chen, “The cocktail party problem,” Neural\n685\tComputation, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 1875–1902, 2005.\n686\t[4] J. A. O’sullivan, A. J. Power, N. Mesgarani, S. Rajaram, J. J.\n687\tFoxe, B. G. Shinn-Cunningham, M. Slaney, S. A. Shamma, and\n688\tE. C. Lalor, “Attentional selection in a cocktail party environment\n689\tcan be decoded from single-trial EEG,” Cerebral Cortex, vol. 25,\n690\tno. 7, pp. 1697–1706, 2015.\n691\t[5] C. Puffay, B. Accou, L. Bollens, M. J. Monesi, J. Vanthornhout,\n692\tH. Van Hamme, and T. Francart, “Relating EEG to continuous\n693\tspeech using deep neural networks: A review,” Journal of Neural\n694\tEngineering, vol. 20, p. 041003, 2023.\n695\t[6] A. W. Bronkhorst, “The cocktail-party problem revisited: Early\n696\tprocessing and selection of multi-talker speech,” Attention, Perception, and Psychophysics, vol. 77, no. 5, pp. 1465–1487, 2015.\n697\t[7] E. S. Teoh and E. C. Lalor, “EEG decoding of the target speaker\n698\tin a cocktail party scenario: Considerations regarding dynamic\n699\tswitching of talker location,” Journal of Neural Engineering,\n700\tvol. 16, no. 3, p. 036017, 2019.\n701\t[8] G. Ivucic, S. Pahuja, D. Da Silva, and T. Schultz, “Selective auditory attention decoding in naturalistic conversations using EEGbased speech envelope tracking in multi-speaker environments,”\n702\tin Proceedings of the INTERSPEECH, 2025, pp. 2925–2929.\n703\t[9] I. Van de Ryck, N. Heintz, I. Rotaru, S. Geirnaert, A. Bertrand,\n704\tand T. Francart, “EEG-based decoding of auditory attention to\n705\tconversations with turn-taking speakers,” Hearing Research, vol.\n706\t471, p. 109539, 2026.\n707\t[10] S. Geirnaert, S. Vandecappelle, E. Alickovic, A. De Cheveigne,\n708\tE. Lalor, B. T. Meyer, S. Miran, T. Francart, and A. Bertrand,\n709\t“Electroencephalography-based auditory attention decoding: Toward neurosteered hearing devices,” IEEE Signal Processing\n710\tMagazine, vol. 38, no. 4, pp. 89–102, 2021.\n711\t[11] L. S. Schiavon, J. C. M. Bermudez, and M. H. Costa, “Impact of\n712\tauditory attention decoding accuracy on noise reduction systems\n713\tfor hearing aids,” Biomedical Signal Processing and Control, vol.\n714\t113, p. 109162, 2026.\n715\t[12] C. Fan, H. Zhang, Q. Ni, J. Zhang, J. Tao, J. Zhou, J. Yi, Z. Lv,\n716\tand X. Wu, “Seeing helps hearing: A multi-modal dataset and a\n717\tmamba-based dual branch parallel network for auditory attention\n718\tdecoding,” Information Fusion, p. 102946, 2025.\n719\t[13] C. Fan, X. Yang, H. Zhang, Y. Chen, L. Li, J. Zhou, and Z. Lv,\n720\t“ListenNet: A lightweight spatio-temporal enhancement nested\n721\tnetwork for auditory attention detection,” in Proceedings of the\n722\t2025 International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI), 2025, pp. 4137–4145.\n723\t\n724\t[14] Y. Ding, L. Wang, J. Lu, Z. Lin, and F. Chen, “EEG-based\n725\tauditory attention decoding for speaker identification under\n726\tmixed-speech hearing-assistive conditions,” IEEE Transactions\n727\ton Biomedical Engineering, 2025.\n728\t[15] G. Buzsáki, Rhythms of the Brain. Oxford University Press,\n729\t2006.\n730\t[16] I. Rotaru, S. Geirnaert, N. Heintz, I. Van de Ryck, A. Bertrand,\n731\tand T. Francart, “What are we really decoding? Unveiling biases\n732\tin EEG-based decoding of the spatial focus of auditory attention,”\n733\tJournal of Neural Engineering, vol. 21, no. 1, p. 016017, 2024.\n734\t[17] J. Hjortkjær, D. D. Wong, A. Catania, J. Märcher-Rørsted, E. Ceolini, S. A. Fuglsang, I. Kiselev, G. Di Liberto, S.-C. Liu, T. Dau\n735\tet al., “Real-time control of a hearing instrument with EEG-based\n736\tattention decoding,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 22, no. 1,\n737\tp. 016027, 2025.\n738\t[18] Z. Qiu, J. Gu, D. Yao, J. Li, and Y. Yan, “Enhancing spatial auditory attention decoding with wavelet-based prototype training,”\n739\tBiomedical Signal Processing and Control, vol. 100, p. 107130,\n740\t2025.\n741\t[19] Y. Ding, L. Wang, X. Wang, and F. Chen, “Developing lightweight\n742\tmodels with data optimization for attending speaker identity from\n743\tEEG without spatial information,” in Proceedings of the 2025\n744\tIEEE International Conference on Engineering in Medicine and\n745\tBiology Society (EMBC), 2025, pp. 1–4.\n746\t[20] S. Cai, R. Zhang, and H. Li, “Robust decoding of the auditory attention from EEG recordings through graph convolutional\n747\tnetworks,” in Proceedings of the 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n748\t2024, pp. 2320–2324.\n749\t[21] Z. Zhang, A. Thwaites, A. Woolgar, B. Moore, and C. Zhang,\n750\t“SWIM: Short-window CNN integrated with mamba for EEGbased auditory spatial attention decoding,” in Proceedings of the\n751\t2024 IEEE International Conference on Spoken Language Technology Workshop (SLT), 2024, pp. 1031–1038.\n752\t[22] Y. Zhang, J. Lu, F. Chen, H. Du, X. Gao, and Z. Lin, “Multi-class\n753\tdecoding of attended speaker direction using electroencephalogram and audio spatial spectrum,” IEEE Transactions on Neural\n754\tSystems and Rehabilitation Engineering, vol. 33, pp. 2892–2903,\n755\t2025.\n756\t[23] L. Bollens, B. Accou, H. Van Hamme, and T. Francart, “Contrastive representation learning with Transformers for robust auditory EEG decoding,” Scientific Reports, vol. 15, no. 1, p. 28744,\n757\t2025.\n758\t[24] A. Baevski, Y. Zhou, A. Mohamed, and M. Auli, “wav2vec\n759\t2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n760\t(NeurIPS), vol. 33, pp. 12 449–12 460, 2020.\n761\t[25] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N.\n762\tGomez, Ł. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin, “Attention is all you need,”\n763\tAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS),\n764\tvol. 30, p. 5998–6008, 2017.\n765\t[26] Y. Ding, S. Yu, X. Chen, X. Wang, Y. Ban, and F. Chen, “SAASDNet: An EEG-based streaming auditory attention switch decoding\n766\tnetwork for self-initiated attention switching in mixed speech,”\n767\tin Proceedings of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on\n768\tAcoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2026, pp.\n769\t7016–7020.\n770\t[27] Y. Ding, S. Yu, X. Wang, Y. Ban, and F. Chen, “MS-AASD: An\n771\tEEG dataset for self-initiated auditory attention switch decoding\n772\tin mixed speech,” Zenodo, Sep. 2025. [Online]. Available:\n773\thttps://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17149387\n774\t[28] S. C. Warby, S. L. Wendt, P. Welinder, E. G. S. Munk, O. Carrillo, H. B. D. Sorensen, P. Jennum, P. E. Peppard, P. Perona, and\n775\tE. Mignot, “Sleep-spindle detection: Crowdsourcing and evaluating performance of experts, non-experts and automated methods,”\n776\tNature Methods, vol. 11, no. 4, pp. 385–392, 2014.\n777\t[29] V. Shah, M. Golmohammadi, I. Obeid, and J. Picone, “Objective\n778\tevaluation metrics for automatic classification of EEG events,”\n779\tin Biomedical signal processing: innovation and applications.\n780\tSpringer, 2021, pp. 223–255.\n781\t\n782\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c54c7704-7c55-4ef9-b6d2-c3ba122c790b", "uuid": "7a2f30df-490d-4a2d-b4b1-58909620bffa", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T14:23:29.949Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sgad_paper.txt", "content": "1.39\n1.25\n1.46\n1.49\n1.31\n\n76.7\n75.2\n75.8\n76.1\n75.9\n\n62.3\n48.5\n56.8\n53.7\n60.4\n\n1.42\n1.28\n1.49\n1.52\n1.32\n\nformance, underscoring the importance of learnable gating for\nadaptive smoothing. Furthermore, eliminating the auxiliary loss\nLstate reduces performance due to the lack of explicit transition\nsupervision, while removing Lsmooth similarly leads to performance degradation by weakening temporal regularization. Collectively, these findings highlight the complementary roles of\ntemporal context, adaptive gating, and auxiliary supervision,\nconfirming the importance of explicit transition-state modeling\nfor robust sequential decision-making.\n4.2. Comparison of evaluation protocols\n\n4. Results and Discussion\n4.1. Performance and ablation analysis\nTable 2 compares SGAD with three baseline decision frameworks across two encoders and six evaluation protocols. WD\nachieves the lowest SDL of 0.89 s by operating independently\non each window, but its Sw-F1 remains low at 29.0%, indicating limited robustness in tracking attention transitions. PBD\nand EBD improve temporal consistency, increasing the mean\nAcc from 77.4% to 78.6% and 79.0%, respectively, while raising Sw-F1 to 45.6% and 53.7%. However, their fixed smoothing increases system inertia, resulting in higher SDL values of\n1.23 s and 1.38 s. In contrast, SGAD achieves a better tradeoff among Acc, Sw-F1, and SDL, improving the mean Acc to\n79.8% and Sw-F1 to 67.3% while maintaining a lower SDL\nof 1.18 s compared with EBD. This balanced improvement is\nconsistently observed across all experimental settings. Overall, incorporating transition-state information into the recursive\nupdate enables SGAD to enhance switch detection performance\nwithout the substantial latency penalty inherent in fixed smoothing, thereby enabling more adaptive decision-making.\nTable 3 reports the ablation results under the LOSSO protocol. Replacing the CSD with a single-window state output markedly reduces Sw-F1 (e.g., from 62.3% to 48.5% for\nFCTNet), while achieving a lower SDL of 1.28 s, highlighting the critical role of temporal context in transition estimation. Removing the AGM and substituting the learnable gate\nwith a fixed mapping based on (1 − ŝt ) also degrades per-\n\nPerformance variations across the six protocols demonstrate the\nimpact of generalization constraints on decoding performance.\nIn both subject-dependent and subject-independent settings, accuracy declines as additional constraints on audio content and\nspeaker identity are imposed. For example, under subjectdependent evaluation, the accuracy of FCTNet with the SGAD\nframework decreases from 85.6% (LOTO) to 82.9% (LOAO)\nand 80.5% (LOSpO), with a comparable pattern observed under\nsubject-independent evaluation. Notably, LOSSO, which we introduce as the most stringent protocol, jointly enforces unseensubject and unseen-speaker conditions, resulting in the lowest\naccuracy of 76.7%. These results highlight the increased difficulty of AASD under realistic cross-condition generalization\nand emphasize the importance of rigorous evaluation protocols\nfor practical neuro-steered hearing systems.\n\n5. Conclusion\nThis study addresses decision instability and evaluation bias in\ndynamic AASD by introducing a state-guided adaptive decision\n(SGAD) framework together with structured hierarchical evaluation protocols. By adaptively modulating temporal smoothing according to inferred transition states, SGAD mitigates the\ntrade-off between stability and responsiveness. The consistent\nperformance degradation observed under increasingly stringent\nprotocols further highlights the importance of comprehensive\nevaluation across audio, speaker, and subject dimensions. Together, these contributions advance the development of reliable\nneuro-steered hearing systems in dynamic environments.\n\n\f6. Acknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the National Key Research\nand Development Program of China (2025YFF0518003,\n2023YFF1203502), the National Natural Science Foundation of\nChina (62371217), and the Center for Computational Science\nand Engineering at Southern University of Science and Technology.\n\n7. Generative AI Use Disclosure\nAll (co-)authors are responsible and accountable for the work\nand content of the paper, and consent to its submission. Generative AI tools were used for editing and polishing manuscripts,\nbut were not used for producing a significant part of the\nmanuscript.\n\n8. References\n[1] E. C. Cherry, “Some experiments on the recognition of speech,\nwith one and with two ears,” Journal of the Acoustical Society of\nAmerica, vol. 25, no. 5, pp. 975–979, 1953.\n[2] N. Ding and J. Z. Simon, “Neural coding of continuous speech in\nauditory cortex during monaural and dichotic listening,” Journal\nof Neurophysiology, vol. 107, no. 1, pp. 78–89, 2012.\n[3] S. Haykin and Z. Chen, “The cocktail party problem,” Neural\nComputation, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 1875–1902, 2005.\n[4] J. A. O’sullivan, A. J. Power, N. Mesgarani, S. Rajaram, J. J.\nFoxe, B. G. Shinn-Cunningham, M. Slaney, S. A. Shamma, and\nE. C. Lalor, “Attentional selection in a cocktail party environment\ncan be decoded from single-trial EEG,” Cerebral Cortex, vol. 25,\nno. 7, pp. 1697–1706, 2015.\n[5] C. Puffay, B. Accou, L. Bollens, M. J. Monesi, J. Vanthornhout,\nH. Van Hamme, and T. Francart, “Relating EEG to continuous\nspeech using deep neural networks: A review,” Journal of Neural\nEngineering, vol. 20, p. 041003, 2023.\n[6] A. W. Bronkhorst, “The cocktail-party problem revisited: Early\nprocessing and selection of multi-talker speech,” Attention, Perception, and Psychophysics, vol. 77, no. 5, pp. 1465–1487, 2015.\n[7] E. S. Teoh and E. C. Lalor, “EEG decoding of the target speaker\nin a cocktail party scenario: Considerations regarding dynamic\nswitching of talker location,” Journal of Neural Engineering,\nvol. 16, no. 3, p. 036017, 2019.\n[8] G. Ivucic, S. Pahuja, D. Da Silva, and T. Schultz, “Selective auditory attention decoding in naturalistic conversations using EEGbased speech envelope tracking in multi-speaker environments,”\nin Proceedings of the INTERSPEECH, 2025, pp. 2925–2929.\n[9] I. Van de Ryck, N. Heintz, I. Rotaru, S. Geirnaert, A. Bertrand,\nand T. Francart, “EEG-based decoding of auditory attention to\nconversations with turn-taking speakers,” Hearing Research, vol.\n471, p. 109539, 2026.\n[10] S. Geirnaert, S. Vandecappelle, E. Alickovic, A. De Cheveigne,\nE. Lalor, B. T. Meyer, S. Miran, T. Francart, and A. Bertrand,\n“Electroencephalography-based auditory attention decoding: Toward neurosteered hearing devices,” IEEE Signal Processing\nMagazine, vol. 38, no. 4, pp. 89–102, 2021.\n[11] L. S. Schiavon, J. C. M. Bermudez, and M. H. Costa, “Impact of\nauditory attention decoding accuracy on noise reduction systems\nfor hearing aids,” Biomedical Signal Processing and Control, vol.\n113, p. 109162, 2026.\n[12] C. Fan, H. Zhang, Q. Ni, J. Zhang, J. Tao, J. Zhou, J. Yi, Z. Lv,\nand X. Wu, “Seeing helps hearing: A multi-modal dataset and a\nmamba-based dual branch parallel network for auditory attention\ndecoding,” Information Fusion, p. 102946, 2025.\n[13] C. Fan, X. Yang, H. Zhang, Y. Chen, L. Li, J. Zhou, and Z. Lv,\n“ListenNet: A lightweight spatio-temporal enhancement nested\nnetwork for auditory attention detection,” in Proceedings of the\n2025 International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI), 2025, pp. 4137–4145.\n\n[14] Y. Ding, L. Wang, J. Lu, Z. Lin, and F. Chen, “EEG-based\nauditory attention decoding for speaker identification under\nmixed-speech hearing-assistive conditions,” IEEE Transactions\non Biomedical Engineering, 2025.\n[15] G. Buzsáki, Rhythms of the Brain. Oxford University Press,\n2006.\n[16] I. Rotaru, S. Geirnaert, N. Heintz, I. Van de Ryck, A. Bertrand,\nand T. Francart, “What are we really decoding? Unveiling biases\nin EEG-based decoding of the spatial focus of auditory attention,”\nJournal of Neural Engineering, vol. 21, no. 1, p. 016017, 2024.\n[17] J. Hjortkjær, D. D. Wong, A. Catania, J. Märcher-Rørsted, E. Ceolini, S. A. Fuglsang, I. Kiselev, G. Di Liberto, S.-C. Liu, T. Dau\net al., “Real-time control of a hearing instrument with EEG-based\nattention decoding,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 22, no. 1,\np. 016027, 2025.\n[18] Z. Qiu, J. Gu, D. Yao, J. Li, and Y. Yan, “Enhancing spatial auditory attention decoding with wavelet-based prototype training,”\nBiomedical Signal Processing and Control, vol. 100, p. 107130,\n2025.\n[19] Y. Ding, L. Wang, X. Wang, and F. Chen, “Developing lightweight\nmodels with data optimization for attending speaker identity from\nEEG without spatial information,” in Proceedings of the 2025\nIEEE International Conference on Engineering in Medicine and\nBiology Society (EMBC), 2025, pp. 1–4.\n[20] S. Cai, R. Zhang, and H. Li, “Robust decoding of the auditory attention from EEG recordings through graph convolutional\nnetworks,” in Proceedings of the 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n2024, pp. 2320–2324.\n[21] Z. Zhang, A. Thwaites, A. Woolgar, B. Moore, and C. Zhang,\n“SWIM: Short-window CNN integrated with mamba for EEGbased auditory spatial attention decoding,” in Proceedings of the\n2024 IEEE International Conference on Spoken Language Technology Workshop (SLT), 2024, pp. 1031–1038.\n[22] Y. Zhang, J. Lu, F. Chen, H. Du, X. Gao, and Z. Lin, “Multi-class\ndecoding of attended speaker direction using electroencephalogram and audio spatial spectrum,” IEEE Transactions on Neural\nSystems and Rehabilitation Engineering, vol. 33, pp. 2892–2903,\n2025.\n[23] L. Bollens, B. Accou, H. Van Hamme, and T. Francart, “Contrastive representation learning with Transformers for robust auditory EEG decoding,” Scientific Reports, vol. 15, no. 1, p. 28744,\n2025.\n[24] A. Baevski, Y. Zhou, A. Mohamed, and M. Auli, “wav2vec\n2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n(NeurIPS), vol. 33, pp. 12 449–12 460, 2020.\n[25] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N.\nGomez, Ł. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin, “Attention is all you need,”\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS),\nvol. 30, p. 5998–6008, 2017.\n[26] Y. Ding, S. Yu, X. Chen, X. Wang, Y. Ban, and F. Chen, “SAASDNet: An EEG-based streaming auditory attention switch decoding\nnetwork for self-initiated attention switching in mixed speech,”\nin Proceedings of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on\nAcoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2026, pp.\n7016–7020.\n[27] Y. Ding, S. Yu, X. Wang, Y. Ban, and F. Chen, “MS-AASD: An\nEEG dataset for self-initiated auditory attention switch decoding\nin mixed speech,” Zenodo, Sep. 2025. [